Preamble

The GNU Affero General Public License is a free, copyleft license for
software and other kinds of works, specifically designed to ensure
cooperation with the community in the case of network server software.

The licenses for most software and other practical tools are designed
to take away your freedom to share and change the software. By contrast,
the GNU General Public License is intended to guarantee your freedom to
share and change all versions of a program--to make sure it remains free
software for all its users.

When we speak of free software, we are referring to freedom of use, not
price. Our General Public Licenses are designed to make sure that you
have the freedom to distribute copies of free software (and charge for
this service if you wish), that you receive source code or can get it
if you want it, that you can change the software and use pieces of it
in new free programs; and that you are informed that you can do these
things.

For developers' protection, the GPL clearly explains that there is no
warranty for this free software. For both users' and creators' sake,
the GPL requires that modified versions be marked as changed, so that
their problems will not be attributed erroneously to authors of
previous versions.

Some devices are designed to deny users access to install or run
modified versions of the software inside them, although the manufacturer
can do so. This is fundamentally incompatible with the aim of protecting
users' freedom to change the software. The systematic pattern of such
abuse occurs in the case of products most often used by corporations to
limit users' freedom most. Therefore, this is the particular danger that
motivates our version of the GPL for free software that runs on
commonly used personal computers.

The Affero GPL applies to network server software. When we spoke of
protecting users' freedom to change the software, we referred to versions
meant for them to run on their own machines. When a program's users
interact with it remotely through a computer network, the version running
on the server can be important for all users even though they do not
control it directly. The GNU Affero General Public License takes account
of this situation. When you modify covered software and make your
modifications available to the public in some way (even if you don't
distribute them), the covered software should make it easy for its users
to get the source of your modifications.

For details on the full license, see: https://www.gnu.org/licenses/agpl-3.0.txt
